    "proxyDiagnosisAndRepair": {
      "go_version": "go1.25.12 linux/amd64",
      "original_full_suite_failure": "/workspace/scratch/725a395bb84b/checks/final-go-first-attempt.jsonl",
      "classification": "Pre-existing production HTTP/1 early-response race, plus an overstrict completion assumption in the original SSE fixture; not a UHP regression.",
      "baseline_comparison": "Before the fix, git diff --no-index baseline-test/internal/observeproxy work/rundesk-v0.52.0/internal/observeproxy exited 0 with no diff. Original SSE test failed in both trees at count=200; baseline also failed under -race at count=20.",
      "root_cause": "The upstream can respond before Transport starts reading the incoming request body. Without EnableFullDuplex, the Go HTTP/1 server drains and closes its original reqBody when the first response is flushed, bypassing the capture wrapper and competing with Transport. A subsequent request-body read error closes the upstream connection, truncating SSE.",
      "fix": "Enable http.NewResponseController(w).EnableFullDuplex() after route validation in Proxy.serveHTTP. No buffering or replay is introduced.",
      "precise_regression": "headerGateConn writes upstream headers but blocks Write returning until the downstream receives the first SSE line. Thus Transport cannot start reading request body before the response is flushed. Upstream responds first, then consumes and verifies the request body before emitting DONE. Before fix: 3/3 fail; final state: 200 normal + 200 race passes.",
      "fixture_correction": "Original SSE fixture now reads and validates request body AFTER the first Flush, before finishing. A server that never reads the body may validly finish its response before Transport sends the body; incomplete request capture in that case is truthful. This is not a pre-read that hides the production race.",
      "final_validation": "Two SSE tests each pass 200 normal and 200 race repetitions (800 final targeted invocations). All 20 observeproxy top-level tests pass under race count=5. The original full-suite failure is preserved; this summary does not claim a fresh full normal suite passed.",
